## Usage

### Authentication Composable

The main composable [`useOnionAuth()`](app/composables/useOnionAuth.ts) provides authentication functionality:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <div v-if="isAuthenticated">
      <h1>Welcome {{ userSession?.user.profile?.name }}!</h1>
      <p v-if="userSession?.user.roles">Roles: {{ userSession.user.roles.join(', ') }}</p>
      <p v-if="userSession?.user.groups">Groups: {{ userSession.user.groups.join(', ') }}</p>
      <button @click="logout()">Logout</button>
    </div>
    <div v-else>
      <button @click="login('microsoft')">Login with Microsoft</button>
      <button @click="login('auth0')">Login with Auth0</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>

<script setup>
const { userSession, isAuthenticated, login, logout } = useOnionAuth()
</script>
```

#### API Reference

- **`userSession`**: Reactive user session data (readonly)
- **`isAuthenticated`**: Computed boolean indicating login status
- **`login(provider, options?)`**: Login function with provider selection
- **`logout(options?)`**: Logout function with optional redirect

### Authentication Middleware

The global middleware [`auth.global.ts`](app/middleware/auth.global.ts) is automatically active. To make a page public:

```vue
<script setup>
// Public page (no authentication required)
definePageMeta({
  public: true
})
</script>
```

The middleware automatically:
- Protects all routes by default
- Allows public routes with `public: true` meta
- Skips authentication routes (`/auth/*`)
- Handles login/logout routes
- Stores redirect URLs in cookies for post-login navigation

## Roles and Groups Support

### Overview

The onion-auth layer now supports extracting and managing user roles and groups from authentication providers, particularly Microsoft Azure AD. This enables role-based access control and permission management in your applications.

### Microsoft Azure Configuration

To enable roles and groups in Microsoft Azure AD:

#### 1. Application Roles
1. In Azure AD, go to **App registrations** > Your app > **App roles**
2. Create application roles (e.g., `admin`, `editor`, `viewer`)
3. Assign these roles to users in **Enterprise applications**

#### 2. Group Claims
1. In **Token configuration**, add the `groups` claim
2. Configure **Group claims** in the **Token configuration**
3. Ensure the application has the necessary permissions

#### 3. Required Scopes
```typescript
scope: ["openid", "profile", "email", "User.Read"]
```

### Data Structure

Roles and groups are automatically extracted from the ID token and included in the user session:

```typescript
interface User {
  username: string
  profile: {
    name?: string
    email?: string
    picture?: string
    sub?: string
  }
  picture?: string
  roles?: string[]    // Application roles from Azure AD
  groups?: string[]   // Group memberships from Azure AD
}
```

### Usage Examples

#### Accessing Roles and Groups
```vue
